!id: jBpsQfqdqz
!front: State the Mean Value Theorem for Integrals.
!back:
If \( f \) is continuous on \( [a,b] \), then there exists \( c\in[a,b] \) such that
\[ \int_a^b f(x)\,dx=f(c)(b-a). \]
Equivalently, \( f(c) \) equals the average value \( \frac{1}{b-a}\int_a^b f \), assuming \( a<b \).
!extra:
Geometrically: the area under the curve equals the area of a rectangle of height \( f(c) \) and width \( b-a \).

![Mean Value Theorem for Integrals](img/mean_value_theorem_integral.svg)
~~~

!id: rLdbzPXWhT
!front: State the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus, Part 1.
!back:
If \( f \) is continuous on \( [a,b] \) and
\[ F(x)=\int_a^x f(t)\,dt, \]
then \( F \) is differentiable on \( (a,b) \), and
\[ F'(x)=f(x) \]
for every \( x\in(a,b) \).
~~~

!id: xyQeQcoPe5
!front: Derive the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus, Part 1, from the Mean Value Theorem for Integrals.
!back:
Let:

- \( F(x)=\int_a^x f(t)\,dt \), where \( f \) is continuous on \( [a,b] \)
- \( x\in(a,b) \)
- \( h\ne0 \) small enough that \( x+h\in(a,b) \)

To prove: \( F'(x)=f(x) \).

- By additivity,
\[ F(x+h)-F(x)=\int_x^{x+h} f(t)\,dt. \]
- By the Mean Value Theorem for Integrals, there is a point \( c_h \) between \( x \) and \( x+h \) such that
\[ \int_x^{x+h} f(t)\,dt=f(c_h)h. \]
- Hence
\[ \frac{F(x+h)-F(x)}{h}=f(c_h). \]
- As \( h\to0 \), \( c_h\to x \), so continuity gives \( f(c_h)\to f(x) \).
- Therefore \( F'(x)=f(x) \).
~~~

!id: 33XsHNIEWI
!front: State the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus, Part 2.
!back:
If \( f \) is continuous on \( [a,b] \) and \( F \) is an antiderivative of \( f \) on that interval, then
\[ \int_a^b f(x)\,dx=F(b)-F(a). \]
That is, a definite integral can be evaluated by any antiderivative of its integrand.
~~~

!id: fw0ZcECXO4
!front: Derive the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus, Part 2, from Part 1.
!back:
Let:

- \( G(x)=\int_a^x f(t)\,dt \), with \( f \) continuous on \( [a,b] \)
- \( F \) be any antiderivative of \( f \) on \( [a,b] \)

To prove: \( \int_a^b f(x)\,dx=F(b)-F(a) \).

- By FTC1, \( G'(x)=f(x) \) for \( x\in(a,b) \).
- Since \( F'(x)=f(x) \), we have \( (F-G)'=0 \) on \( (a,b) \).
- By the Mean Value Theorem, \( F-G \) is constant on \( [a,b] \). Thus \( F(x)=G(x)+C \) for some constant \( C \).
- Therefore
  \[
  \begin{align*}
    F(b)-F(a)
        &= \big(G(b)+C\big)-\big(G(a)+C\big) \\
        &= G(b)-G(a) \\
        &= \int_a^b f(t)\,dt-\int_a^a f(t)\,dt \\
        &= \int_a^b f(t)\,dt.
  \end{align*}
\]
!extra: Uses that a function with derivative identically \( 0 \) on an interval is constant there — itself a consequence of the Mean Value Theorem.
~~~

!id: TD5D0QhdCM
!front: State the net change theorem for a quantity \( Q(t) \) with continuous rate of change \( Q'(t) \) on \( [a,b] \).
!back:
If \( Q' \) is continuous on \( [a,b] \), then
\[ \int_a^b Q'(t)\,dt = Q(b)-Q(a). \]
The integral of the instantaneous rate of change is the total signed change in the original quantity.
~~~

!id: o07ksA9LI9
!front: How does the net change theorem relate to the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus, Part 2?
!back:
It is FTC2 read in applied language. Take \( f=Q' \) and \( F=Q \). Then FTC2 says
\[ \int_a^b Q'(t)\,dt=Q(b)-Q(a). \]
The mathematics is the same; the interpretation is that integrating a rate recovers accumulated net change.
~~~
